WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.859
With Avengers: Age Of Ultron out on Blu-ray
&amp; DVD now, let’s take a look at the 50 of

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.050
the best Easter Eggs and references found
in the second Avengers outing.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.849
If you haven’t seen Age Of Ultron yet, just
be aware there are spoilers ahead for the

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:17.519
film and future Marvel movies.

00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:21.800
In Age of Ultron, Captain America’s shield
has been upgraded with magnetic elements so

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.029
he can call it back to him.
Avengers comics from the early 60s also show

00:00:26.029 --> 00:00:29.550
Tony Stark upgraded Cap’s shield in a similar
manner.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:34.370
The new shield also echoes Stark’s ability
to call his Iron Man suits to him, as we saw

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:40.940
in Iron Man 3, and after Loki pushed him out
of Stark Tower in The Avengers movie.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.120
During Captain America’s face-off with Ultron
on top of a speeding truck, there’s a nice

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.149
nod to the late 1970s Captain America TV movies
starring Reb Brown.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.989
In Age Of Ultron, Cap’s shield falls down
to the road where Black Widow scoops it up

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.789
and places it on the front of her motorbike,
echoing the look of Reb Brown’s 70s bike

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:05.040
which featured a round windshield reminiscent
of Cap’s shield.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.570
During the Avengers’ attack on Strucker’s
Hydra Base, Thor hits Captain America’s

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.820
shield with his hammer, creating a sonic shockwave
that knocks their enemies on their asses and

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.280
takes out their tank.
This is a cool call-back with a twist to The

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.960
Avengers movie where The Asgardian god and
the US super-soldier first saw the effect

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.939
of their hammer and shield combo in the movies.
However, that time they were on opposing sides

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:32.130
in an argument over Loki, and Cap demanded
Thor put down his hammer… which he did as

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.729
he flew through the air, smashing it into
Cap’s vibranium shield, causing a shockwave

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:41.450
that razed the forest around them!
For more on Cap’s shield and all things

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.909
Avengers, check out my video 12 Things You
Didn’t Know About the Avengers: Age Of Ultron.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:53.110
The epic slow-mo shot that shows the Avengers
in mid-air poses as they attack Strucker’s

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.380
men during their raid of his Hydra Research
Base in Sokovia feels like something that

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.340
could have been torn straight from the pages
of a comic book.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.369
Director Joss Whedon said that what he wants
to say with that shot that is, “Here’s

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:07.340
a comic book movie, and here’s the feeling
that you came to get.”’

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:13.690
When Tony Stark enters the HYDRA base, he
discovers the remains of a Leviathan, one

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.410
of the enormous beasts used as alien warships
during the Battle of New York in the first

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:22.390
Avengers movie.
Joss Whedon and Zak Penn created the Leviathans

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:28.030
especially for the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
however, the word Leviathan does crop up elsewhere

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:35.160
in the comics and in Agent Carter as the name
of a secret Soviet organisation.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.330
Tony Stark’s dark vision of the Avengers
defeated reminds me of the nightmares he had

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.380
in Iron Man 3 following the PTSD he suffered
after the Battle Of New York.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:51.150
Stan Lee couldn’t budge Thor’s hammer
in the first Thor movie, and in Age Of Ultron

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.050
he couldn’t keep up with Thor’s drinking
either!

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:59.010
Playing a World War 2 veteran, Lee tries to
prove Thor wrong that his special Asgardian

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:04.450
liquor is ‘not meant for mortal men’,
but he ends up sozzled, slurring his catchphrase

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:10.230
Excelsior, which is Latin for ‘Ever Upward’
and is also the state motto of New York.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.790
New York-born Lee has used the phrase Excelsior
repeatedly over the years in his columns for

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:19.790
Marvel comics and in the title of his autobiography.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:24.620
The liquor Thor says mortals can’t handle
‘was aged for a thousand years in the barrels

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.920
built from the wreck of Brunnhilde’s fleet.’
Brunnhilde is a character in Marvel’s comics.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.480
She was picked by Thor’s father Odin to
lead a host of warrior goddesses known as

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.270
Valkyrior.
Brunnhilde was considered as a potential character

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.740
for Thor: The Dark World, though didn’t
make the cut.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:45.260
Perhaps this mention is a sign we’ll see
her in Thor: Ragnarok, a storyline that includes

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.930
Brunnhilde in the comics.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:54.630
During the Avengers’ party, Anthony Mackie’s
character Sam Wilson, aka Falcon, tells Captain

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:58.800
America that he’s ‘happy chasing cold
leads on our missing person’s case.’

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.520
Which is a reference to Cap’s best friend
and fellow army man Bucky Barnes, who became

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.620
the Winter Soldier assassin after experiments
by Hydra, and then disappeared after saving

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.770
Cap at the end of the second Captain America
movie.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:17.060
Last time we saw Bucky, he was at the Smithsonian
Museum, dressed in civilian clothes and looking

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.940
at the ‘Bucky Barnes’ display in the Captain
America exhibit.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:27.600
During the Avengers’ after-party, when Steve
Rogers tries to lift Thor’s hammer, he can’t

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.790
quite manage it but he does move it a little.
Which is a nod to the comic books where Cap

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:38.220
has been deemed worthy to lift the Asgardian
god’s hammer, for example, in issue 390

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.480
of The Mighty Thor back in 1988.
And in the movie when Natasha Romanov refuses

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.760
to take her turn to lift the hammer, her remark
‘that’s not a question I need answered’,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.880
is a sly allusion to the fact that Black Widow
already proved herself worthy of wielding

00:04:52.880 --> 00:05:00.840
Thor’s hammer in issue 3 of the 2014 comic
book mini-series What If? Age Of Ultron.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.750
At the end of Age Of Ultron, Captain America
wonders if Thor left his hammer in an elevator

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:10.340
and the elevator moved whether that would
mean the elevator was worthy. Thor laughs

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.210
this off.
But that little exchange recalls the funny

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:19.620
moment in Thor: The Dark World where the Asgardian
god hung his hammer on a coat hook in Jane’s

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:20.420
apartment.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:26.220
Just before the Avengers have their visions,
Thor warns Captain America that Scarlet Witch

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:31.530
has tried to warp his mind, and tells him,
‘Take special care, I doubt a human could

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:37.140
keep her at bay. Fortunately, I am mighty.’
Which is a nice nod to the title of The Mighty

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:43.710
Thor comics as well as the 1960s animated
TV series of the same name!

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:49.460
When Ultron first becomes conscious and starts
trawling for information, we see various images

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.460
from other Marvel movies, including one of
Tony Stark from the beginning of the first

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.170
Iron Man movie.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:06:00.389
While Black Widow goes for her gun during
the Avengers’ first face-off with Ultron,

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:04.670
Tony Stark grabs a fondue fork to battle Ultron’s
bots.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:10.060
That fondue fork is a playful nod to the movie
Captain America: The First Avenger where Tony’s

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:14.620
father Howard Stark says to Peggy Carter,
‘I thought we could stop off in Lucerne

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:19.370
for a late-night fondue.’
Which leads Cap to awkwardly ask Peggy, ‘So,

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:23.180
are you two… do you… fondue?’

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:27.440
When Ultron first appears to the Avengers,
he makes several references in his speech

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.770
to strings and puppets.
Then after Thor smashes him with his hammer,

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:36.660
we hear Ultron sing his very creepy version
of a few lines from the song “I’ve Got

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:42.000
Not Strings”, which featured in the 1940
Disney animated film Pinocchio, about a wooden

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.080
puppet who becomes a real boy. And we even
hear a little clip from the original version

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.620
of the song.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.169
When Ultron first speaks to Scarlet Witch
and Quicksilver, he’s wearing a red cloak,

00:06:54.169 --> 00:06:59.590
which is a reference to his identity in the
comics as the Crimson Cowl.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:04.270
When Ultron tells Scarlett Witch and Quicksilver
that ‘invaders create avengers’, it’s

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:10.180
a nod to the comic-book superhero team The
Invaders, which was co-created by Roy Thomas,

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:16.080
and whose original line-up included Captain
America, Bucky Barnes, and the Sub-Mariner.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.430
Black Widow’s vision of the Red Room gives
us an insight into the painful process that

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:25.490
made her the character we know today, and
through her Graduation Ceremony also provides

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:28.400
a link with Bruce Banner who can’t have
children either.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.110
Natasha’s Red Room vision is a nod to both
Marvel’s comics and the TV series Agent

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:38.800
Carter and their Soviet espionage training
programme which turns young girls into super-spies.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.830
The film’s ballet scenes refer back to the
Black Widow’s origins in the comic books

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:47.540
where, depending on the story arc, she either
trained as a ballerina or was implanted with

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:52.419
false memories to think she was one.
As for Natasha’s enforced sterilisation,

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:58.699
that’s a nod to writer Richard K Morgan’s
Black Widow mini-series.

00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:04.050
During Captain America’s vision, he sees
Peggy Carter in a Second World War ballroom,

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:08.330
and she asks him ‘Are you ready for our
dance?’, after which we see them briefly

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:11.110
dance together.
This recalls the scene in Captain America:

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:15.669
The Winter Soldier when Cap visited an elderly
Peggy in her retirement home.

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:19.650
Peggy was taken aback to see him after so
many years, to which Cap replied, ‘Well,

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.270
I couldn’t leave my best girl. Not when
she owes me a dance.’

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:29.100
Also in the dancehall where Cap’s vision
takes place, there’s a band playing called

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.789
the Roy Thomas Players.
Roy Thomas is a comic book writer and was

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:37.159
Editor-In-Chief of Marvel Comics after Stan
Lee in the 1970s.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:42.990
Thomas co-created many Marvel characters including
Ultron, Vision, and Iron Fist.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:47.430
Thor’s vision set us up for the next Thor
solo movie, Ragnarok.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:52.690
In Norse mythology, Ragnarok means the doom
or twilight of the gods, and it’s also an

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:57.680
apocalyptic event in the comics that results
in numerous significant deaths and the destruction

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.500
of Asgard.
By the way, in the comics, Ragnarok is also

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.950
the name of a Thor clone co-created by Tony
Stark from a strand of the Asgardian god’s

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:09.060
hair.
Which is interesting because in Age Of Ultron,

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:14.100
after Stark fails to lift Thor’s enchanted
hammer, he suggests the handle is imprinted

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:19.690
with a security code matched to Thor’s fingerprints.
So, according Stark’s logic, anyone with

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:24.600
Thor’s fingerprints could wield it. For
example, a clone!

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:29.830
Age Of Ultron marks the fifth film where an
MCU character has grabbed Tony Stark by the

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:32.330
throat.
In the first Avengers movie it was Loki who

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:37.520
did it, in Iron Man 3 it was Guy Pearce’s
character Aldrich Killian, in Iron Man 2 it

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.260
was Black Widow, and in Iron Man it was Jeff
Bridges’ character Obadiah Stane.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:47.630
This time it’s Thor who gets to grips with
Stark’s jugular, and Ultron has a go too!

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:52.270
But, Stark isn’t the only Avenger to get
throttled in this movie: Thor gets similar

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:57.460
treatment at the hands of Ultron as does Vision,
and Captain America, not only gets it from

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:02.540
Ultron but also two of his drones!
And that isn’t all: The Hulk also gets it

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:06.990
in the neck from Iron Man in his Hulkbuster
suit. While, in his vision, Thor gets choked

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.270
by Heimdall.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:14.260
Joss Whedon has said he gave the name Veronica
to Tony Stark’s new armour system which

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:19.510
deploys the Hulkbuster suit via satellite
because Veronica is the opposite of Betty.

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:24.210
Betty is Bruce Banner’s love interest in
the comics and The Incredible Hulk movie.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:29.050
And the Veronica Whedon refers to is likely
Veronica Lodge from Archie Comics who is a

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:33.520
rival to Archie’s girlfriend who also happens
to be called Betty!

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:39.130
It’s possible that Stark’s new armour
system Veronica is also a nod to a Marvel

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:44.460
comics character called Veronica Benning,
a physical therapist employed by Stark Enterprises.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.149
Veronica Benning helps Tony Stark recover
use of one of his hands, which is interesting

00:10:49.149 --> 00:10:56.040
as in Age of Ultron, the Veronica system replaces
Tony Stark’s damaged Hulkbuster arm.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.980
In The Avengers, Loki was locked up in a steel
and glass cage built to contain the Hulk in

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:05.680
case he became a problem on board the helicarrier.
Although that cage was never used for the

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.770
Hulk, in Age Of Ultron Veronica traps him
in another metal cage during the Hulkbuster

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:12.440
scene.
Interestingly, neither Loki nor the Hulk could

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.170
be contained by either cage.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:20.580
When Iron Man tries to calm down the Hulk
during the Hulkbuster sequence, he mentions

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:25.580
Bruce Banner, which only makes the Hulk angrier.
However, Iron Man realises what he’s done

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:28.310
and says to himself, ‘Right! Don’t mention
puny Banner!’

00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:32.779
Which is a call-back to the comic books and
the Hulk’s catchphrase ‘Hulk hate puny

00:11:32.779 --> 00:11:34.980
Banner!’

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:39.070
During the Hulkbuster scene, the Hulk smashes
into a “Crawford” food truck.

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:44.100
The name Crawford is likely a reference to
Dr Geoffrey Crawford, a physics professor

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:48.540
who was Bruce Banner’s mentor before he
turned into the Hulk-like beast Ravage and

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.060
became the Hulk’s enemy.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:56.330
When Bruce Banner hulks out after Scarlet
Witch messes with his mind, Tony Stark in

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:01.630
his Hulkbuster suit tries unsuccessfully to
bring Banner back by hammering the Hulk’s

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:05.089
head while repeating the words “go to sleep,
go to sleep”.

00:12:05.089 --> 00:12:10.029
That was a very Tony Stark interpretation
of the lullaby Black Widow used to talk the

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:13.170
Hulk back down to being Bruce Banner at the
start of the movie.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:17.450
And it’s an interesting role reversal from
The Avengers movie where the Hulk roared at

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:21.690
Stark to bring him back to consciousness after
he fell back to earth through the portal.

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:26.480
Oh, and it also calls to mind the scene where
the Hulk repeatedly smashes Loki into the

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.180
floor of Stark Towers in the first Avengers
movie.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:35.709
During the Hulkbuster scene, the way the Hulk
punches and smashes Iron Man as they blast

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:41.149
up the side of a skyscraper is a call-back
with a twist to the first Avengers movie where

00:12:41.149 --> 00:12:46.270
the Hulk saved Iron Man as they moved down
the side of a skyscraper after Iron Man fell

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:49.399
out of the wormhole.

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:53.839
Remember that powerfully comic moment during
the Battle Of New York when Hulk punched Thor

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:59.200
from the side, sending him flying off screen?
Well, Age Of Ultron gives that a twist during

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:03.649
Iron Man’s battle with the Hulk. This time
we see Stark in his Hulkbuster suit punch

00:13:03.649 --> 00:13:09.209
the Hulk from the side, making the Hulk disappear
off screen!

00:13:09.209 --> 00:13:13.600
Marvel Studios’ President Kevin Feige has
said there’s a Star Wars easter egg which

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.660
involves a character getting an arm or a hand
cut off in all of Marvel's Phase 2 movies.

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:23.010
In the Avengers sequel, not only does Iron
Man’s Hulkbuster shed an arm during his

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:29.200
fight with the Hulk, but arms dealer Ulysses
Klaue also has his arm chopped off by Ultron.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.540
Which is an especially nice touch as Andy
Serkis who plays Klaue is also in Star Wars:

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.890
The Force Awakens!

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:40.380
While we’re on the subject of Andy Serkis’s
character, did you notice the document on

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:44.930
Klaue that the Avengers consult in the movie?
Well, that revealed that he was hired to kill

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:50.920
T’Chaka, the leader of the African nation
Wakanda, to ensure access to the rare metal

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.250
Vibranium.
Vibranium is what Captain America’s shield

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:56.620
is made of and what Ultron buys from Klaue
in this movie.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:01.700
T’Chaka was the father of T’Challa aka
the current Black Panther, who we’ll meet

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:05.990
in Captain America: Civil War before he returns
in his own movie in 2018.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:11.420
So, it looks likely we’ll see Serkis back
on screen as Klaw, the villainous enemy of

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:15.860
the Black Panther, just as he is in the comics.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:21.450
In the salvage yard where Ulysses Klaue is
based, there’s a ship called HMS Churchill,

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:26.240
named after the British wartime Prime Minister
who took over from Neville Chamberlain during

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:29.580
the Second World War.
Chamberlain claimed to have brought “Peace

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:33.750
For Our Time” by arranging a peace treaty
with Hitler, which failed less than a year

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:38.160
later leading to World War Two.
Tony Stark slightly misquotes the phrase as

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:43.769
“peace in our time” to justify his creation
of Ultron, and Ultron later repeats these

00:14:43.769 --> 00:14:45.680
words himself.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:51.470
When Captain America says to Tony Stark, ‘earth’s
mightiest heroes – they pulled us apart

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:56.430
like cotton candy’, it’s a nice nod to
the fact the Avengers are labelled the ‘Earth’s

00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:01.220
Mightiest Heroes’ in the comics, and to
Marvel’s animated TV series, The Avengers:

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:07.170
Earth’s Mightiest Heroes!’, which ran
for two seasons from 2010.

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:11.050
In the movie, Helen Cho is a renowned geneticist
and ally of the Avengers.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:16.120
But in the comics, she’s known as the mother
of teenage genius Amadeus Cho, who’s often

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:21.920
featured in stories alongside the Hulk and
Hercules, and from December 2015 is the lead

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.100
character in Marvel’s Totally Awesome Hulk
series.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:31.880
When Tony Stark sifts through his AI chips
to find a replacement for JARVIS, the one

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:36.769
he picks is marked FRIDAY, which is a reference
to the AI programme that Stark created in

00:15:36.769 --> 00:15:43.269
the comics to act as his secretary.
Also, Stan Lee sometimes referred to 1960s

00:15:43.269 --> 00:15:48.450
Marvel secretary Flo Steinberg as their ‘gal
Friday’.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:52.579
There are also at least 2 other chips that
Tony Stark doesn’t choose. One is marked

00:15:52.579 --> 00:15:58.550
JOCASTA, who in the Marvel comics was a robot
created by Ultron as a mate.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:03.230
The second chip that Tony doesn’t use is
only shown in part, but looks like it could

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:07.420
be labelled "TADASHI".
Tadashi was Hiro's older brother and the creator

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:12.990
of Baymax in Disney’s Big Hero 6, which
was inspired by the Marvel comics of the same

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.019
name.
It’s interesting that there are also lots

00:16:15.019 --> 00:16:20.760
of Iron Man references in Big Hero 6.
For example, Baymax’s first flight is reminiscent

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:24.149
of Tony Stark’s test flight in the first
Iron Man movie.

00:16:24.149 --> 00:16:29.160
And, in the SanFransokyo Nerd Lab, there’s
a cat wearing flying boots a la Iron Man,

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:33.790
a robot assistant that resembles Stark’s
robot helper Dum-E, a student who obviously

00:16:33.790 --> 00:16:38.959
modelled his facial hair on Tony Stark’s,
and what looks like an arc reactor!

00:16:38.959 --> 00:16:44.890
And it’s also possible that the Big Hero
6 nod continues in the Hulkbuster scene where

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:48.820
we see the Hulk and Hulkbuster’s fist bump
send a shockwave through the streets.

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:53.680
Ok, so it wasn’t exactly the same as the
fist bumps between Baymax and Hiro in Big

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:56.680
Hero 6.
But, it is interesting that the Vice President

00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:02.050
of Production at Disney Interactive Studios
has called Baymax the “Hulk meets Iron Man”,

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:06.379
which is what we see in that Age Of Ultron
scene!

00:17:06.379 --> 00:17:11.100
Fox may have the movie rights to Marvel’s
X-Men, but that didn’t stop Age Of Ultron

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:16.609
giving a cheeky nod to the mutant superheroes
after Hawkeye’s motivational speech to Scarlet

00:17:16.609 --> 00:17:20.519
Witch, who in the comics has mutant powers
and parentage herself.

00:17:20.519 --> 00:17:25.639
As Hawkeye leaves Scarlet Witch to fight in
the Battle Of Sokovia, we see a brief shot

00:17:25.639 --> 00:17:30.029
of his hand, out of which seem to pop some
Wolverine-style claws.

00:17:30.029 --> 00:17:34.460
However, they’re actually three sets of
arrows that the bowman’s readying to battle

00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:36.200
some bots.

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:41.539
The SHIELD tech who refused to launch the
Insight helicarriers in Captain America: The

00:17:41.539 --> 00:17:45.200
Winter Soldier has a brief cameo in Age of
Ultron.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:50.269
And like in The Winter Soldier where he was
saved by SHIELD Agent 13 Sharon Carter, in

00:17:50.269 --> 00:17:54.859
this movie it’s former members of SHIELD
Maria Hill and Nick Fury who save him from

00:17:54.859 --> 00:17:58.970
being attacked by one of Ultron’s robots.

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:03.309
The incredible circle shot that showed the
Avengers ready to take on the aliens in the

00:18:03.309 --> 00:18:08.539
Battle Of New York is revisited in Age Of
Ultron’s epic final battle against Ultron

00:18:08.539 --> 00:18:12.039
and his drones in Sokovia.

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:16.690
Ultron fires on the Hulk from his plane during
the Battle Of Sokovia, which leads to the

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:20.739
Hulk leaping on to the plane and throwing
its pilot, Ultron, down to the ground.

00:18:20.739 --> 00:18:25.609
This is a nice call-back to the first Avengers
movie where a pilot also fired on the Hulk

00:18:25.609 --> 00:18:29.739
while he was on the helicarrier which caused
the Hulk to jump on to the plane and throw

00:18:29.739 --> 00:18:32.629
out the pilot.

00:18:32.629 --> 00:18:37.440
As the flying Sokovia starts to fall to the
ground, Vision comes in and saves Scarlet

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:43.369
Witch in a nice little nod to the comics where
Vision and Scarlet Witch marry.

00:18:43.369 --> 00:18:49.059
In Marvel's Ultimates comics, Quicksilver
and Scarlet Witch share an incestuous relationship

00:18:49.059 --> 00:18:54.249
and actors Elizabeth Olsen and Aaron Taylor-Johnson
decided to play a little with that idea so

00:18:54.249 --> 00:18:59.909
in Age of Ultron you'll often see them close
together and holding hands.

00:18:59.909 --> 00:19:04.489
The superhero team’s famous comic book catchphrase,
‘Avengers Assemble!’, never made it into

00:19:04.489 --> 00:19:08.149
the first movie.
And at the end of Age Of Ultron, Captain America

00:19:08.149 --> 00:19:13.399
opens his mouth to address the new Avengers,
but all he gets to say is ‘Avengers’ before

00:19:13.399 --> 00:19:18.249
the camera cuts to the credits as his mouth
forms what looks like the letter ‘a’!

00:19:18.249 --> 00:19:23.600
Funnily enough, Joss Whedon did use the famous
phrase in season 4 episode 1 of Buffy The

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:29.330
Vampire Slayer when Xander talks about getting
the team together.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:33.789
Speaking of Buffy The Vampire Slayer, Whedon
included a blink-and-you’ll-miss-it easter

00:19:33.789 --> 00:19:37.989
egg to his Buffy spin-off show Angel during
Thor’s visions of Asgard.

00:19:37.989 --> 00:19:42.879
In the archways, there are three men wearing
masks – one is a wolf, one is a ram, and

00:19:42.879 --> 00:19:47.679
the other is a hart or deer.
On Angel, Wolfram &amp; Hart is a law firm and

00:19:47.679 --> 00:19:52.480
front for a trio of demons called the Wolf,
the Ram, and the Hart.

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:56.820
There’s also a wink to Whedon’s Firefly
TV series too.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:20:01.269
When the Avengers are in the Quinjet after
the Hydra raid, Tony Stark asks JARVIS to

00:20:01.269 --> 00:20:05.840
take the wheel, and then we see a “Jarvis
is my co-pilot" sticker on the cockpit dash.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:11.039
That’s a nod to Firefly in which Wash is
the pilot of the spaceship Serenity and a

00:20:11.039 --> 00:20:15.590
reference to the popular car license plate
frames and stickers which include the words

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:19.029
‘Wash is my co-pilot.’

00:20:19.029 --> 00:20:23.609
Age Of Ultron also sets up some of the background
for Captain America: Civil War, including

00:20:23.609 --> 00:20:27.799
the conflicting philosophies and burgeoning
opposition between Cap and Iron Man.

00:20:27.799 --> 00:20:32.879
First, there’s Stark’s words early on
that Cap is the boss while ‘I just pay for

00:20:32.879 --> 00:20:35.600
everything, design everything, and make everyone
look cooler’.

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:39.789
Then, there’s Cap and Stark’s clash over
the “suit of armour around the world”

00:20:39.789 --> 00:20:44.249
issue. Stark deliberately hides his Ultron
plans, only sharing them with his Science

00:20:44.249 --> 00:20:47.879
Bro, Banner.
Also, while Stark is worried the Avengers

00:20:47.879 --> 00:20:52.369
won’t be able to deal with another alien
invasion, Cap takes a different position:

00:20:52.369 --> 00:20:56.279
that they’ll win or lose together.
Oh, and of course, Cap, Scarlet Witch and

00:20:56.279 --> 00:21:00.700
Quicksilver all unite to try and stop Stark’s
AI project.

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:05.169
And don’t forget the exchange between Stark
and Cap while they’re chopping wood at Hawkeye’s

00:21:05.169 --> 00:21:10.039
house. Stark says, ‘I don’t trust a guy
without a dark side’, to which Cap replies,

00:21:10.039 --> 00:21:12.629
‘Let’s just say you haven’t seen it
yet.’

00:21:12.629 --> 00:21:17.299
By the way, that next Captain America movie
is inspired by the Marvel comics crossover

00:21:17.299 --> 00:21:20.179
storyline, Civil War.

00:21:20.179 --> 00:21:24.960
In the mid-credits scene, we see Thanos put
on the Infinity Gauntlet and say ‘Fine,

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:28.460
I’ll do it myself.’
Which sets us up nicely for the Avengers:

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:33.799
Infinity War Parts 1 &amp; 2 in which it looks
like Thanos is going to stop relying on others

00:21:33.799 --> 00:21:38.279
like Loki to do his dirty work for him and
start kicking ass and taking Infinity Stones

00:21:38.279 --> 00:21:41.359
himself!
Thanos’ gauntlet is similar to the one in

00:21:41.359 --> 00:21:46.350
Odin’s vault in the first Thor movie, however
this one is for a left hand and has no Infinity

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:50.220
Stones in it yet.
Indeed, Kevin Feige has confirmed there are

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:56.019
2 different gloves in the MCU.
Thanos is the MCU’s big bad. He’s the

00:21:56.019 --> 00:21:59.889
‘someone’ who’s ‘been playing an intricate
game and has made pawns of’ the Avengers,

00:21:59.889 --> 00:22:04.059
to quote Thor from the end of Age of Ultron.
Not that Thor knows it’s Thanos who’s

00:22:04.059 --> 00:22:08.059
the puppet-master at that point.
But thanks to the vision he has, Thor does

00:22:08.059 --> 00:22:12.749
know about the incredible, potentially destructive
power of the 6 Infinity Stones.

00:22:12.749 --> 00:22:19.129
So far, only 4 stones have appeared in the
MCU: the yellow Mind Stone, the purple Power

00:22:19.129 --> 00:22:22.479
Stone, the red Reality Stone, and the blue
Space Stone.

00:22:22.479 --> 00:22:26.299
That means, at some point in the movies running
up to the Infinity Wars, we should see the

00:22:26.299 --> 00:22:28.669
Soul Stone and the Time Stone appear.

00:22:28.669 --> 00:22:33.159
Now, let me know in the comments below: What
other easter eggs and references did you spot

00:22:33.159 --> 00:22:36.999
in Avengers: Age Of Ultron?
If you enjoyed this video, do please share

00:22:36.999 --> 00:22:41.019
it, hit the thumbs-up button and subscribe
for my things you didn't know videos, as well

00:22:41.019 --> 00:22:45.919
as movie reviews and interviews.
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:22:45.919 --> 00:22:47.129
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

